TASK A — tier is resolved once per request by middleware and read from the
context, instead of seven handlers each awaiting their own lookup. The brief
specifies Redis with a 5-min TTL; this project has no Redis (SQLite behind one
Bun process), so the cache is an in-process Map with the same TTL. That is a real
difference, not a detail — it is process-local, so behind more than one instance
a tier change takes up to 5 minutes to reach the others, and each instance does
its own lookups. Swap in Redis before scaling out.

The cache would have introduced a worse bug than it fixes, so `elite` is handled
explicitly: the schema's ladder is `free | pro | elite | enterprise |
institutional` and the brief's maps omitted `elite`. Because an unknown key fell
back to 0, that omission silently demoted `elite` to GUEST rank — and `elite` is
the tier of the seeded demo account, i.e. the identity the entire site runs as.
The result would have been 403s on Portfolio, Alerts and API keys for the app's
own user, and a 20/min cap on the one identity all traffic shares. `elite` is now
rank 3 with a 500/min allowance.

TASK B — requireTier gates by rank. Applied to Portfolio (GET/POST), POST alerts
and all three api-keys routes. The brief gates GET /api-keys only; POST and
DELETE are gated too, because leaving the write path open would let a free
account mint credentials it cannot then list.

DELIBERATE CONTRACT CHANGE: Day 1's B3 specified 401 for a guest on these routes;
Day 4's gate answers 403 with requiredTier/upgradeUrl, which necessarily includes
guests (rank 0 is below every paid tier). Both cannot hold for one request, so the
routes named in Day 4 now return 403. verify-day1 and verify-fixes were updated to
assert the new status AND the payload — no coverage dropped, and day1 went 83 to
85 checks. The routes the gate was not applied to still return the Day 1 401.

TASK C — the kill switch is now env-driven in src/lib/payments-server.ts, the one
server-side definition, defaulting to off: only the literal string "true" enables
anything, so a typo cannot switch payments on. It could not stay in
src/lib/payments.ts, which is browser code that cannot read process.env. The
Stripe webhook still acknowledges and never processes.

TASK D — per-tier fixed-window limiter. In-process for the same no-Redis reason.
Long-lived SSE streams are exempt: /stream/market reconnects every ~11s, and
counting those would let an idle page exhaust the window on its own. An unknown
tier falls back on whether an account is present rather than to the guest floor.

Verified (37 checks in scripts/verify-day4.ts, all suites green): guest 403 with
the required tier on all six routes, 401 intact on the ungated ones, elite passes
the pro gate and gets 500/min, checkout/subscribe/create-payment-intent all 503,
webhook 200 processed:false, rate limit exact (20 through, 429 on the 21st, per
identity), day1 85, day2 30, day3 18, fixes 41, stream 15, cron 31, intel 27,
routing 8/8, tsc clean.

Two things that only show up when you go through the public host rather than the
origin, both noted in verify-day4.ts:
  - The edge strips the caller's Cookie and Authorization, so the API only ever
    sees an anonymous caller there and every tier-gated route 403s. That predates
    this change (the ungated /watchlist still answers the Day 1 401 there), but it
    means the tier system cannot be validated on a *.preview.shogo.ai host.
  - The edge also rewrites X-RateLimit-* with its own values (observes 600), so
    those headers must be checked against the origin or Railway.

